+.. _python_api_relational_merge:
+
+Merging independent integer key spaces
+=====================================
+
+``db.merge_preserving_keys()`` imports a selected graph of related tables while
+allocating new primary keys and rewriting the source's foreign keys:
+
+.. code-block:: python
+
+    result = db.merge_preserving_keys(
+        [("north", "north.db"), ("south", "south.db")],
+        tables=["people", "teams"],
+    )
+
+The destination tables must already exist with matching columns and foreign-key
+declarations. Each table must have a single rowid-aliasing ``INTEGER PRIMARY KEY``;
+foreign keys must use distinct non-PK child fields containing integer or null
+values and reference the selected parents' integer keys. Self-references and
+cycles work. Composite/shared keys, generated columns, virtual tables, source
+triggers and foreign keys leaving the selection are rejected.
+
+Sources are read from consistent read-only SQLite snapshots, including committed
+WAL content. Source IDs are case-sensitive, nonempty and unique in a call. Tables
+follow SQLite identifier casing rules. Sources and tables are processed in sorted
+order, and old IDs in ascending order. Allocated IDs start above the destination's
+current maximum and any AUTOINCREMENT high-water mark. Existing destination rows
+are never replaced. Blobs, nulls and other SQLite values are preserved.
+
+The result contains ``rows_inserted`` and a ``sources`` mapping. Each source has
+``skipped``, per-table ``rows_inserted`` counts, and ``key_map`` containing sorted
+``[old_id, new_id]`` pairs for every selected table. Counts describe this call.
+
+``_sqlite_utils_merge_sources`` records ``source_id``, ``fingerprint`` and
+``tables_json``; ``_sqlite_utils_merge_keys`` records ``source_id``, ``table_name``,
+``old_id`` and ``new_id``. Fingerprints cover the selected definitions and typed
+rows, not SQLite file bytes, so moving a file or checkpointing its WAL does not
+change identity. Reusing an unchanged source ID returns the saved maps and inserts
+nothing. Changing selected schema/data or the table selection raises ``ValueError``.
+Unrelated source tables do not affect identity. Do not edit this metadata.
+
+All new sources, provenance, maps and destination trigger side effects form one
+transaction. Constraint or metadata errors roll the whole operation back. Triggers
+that suppress or alter imported rows are rejected. Foreign keys are checked even
+if enforcement was off initially. Caller transactions use a savepoint, remain open
+and can roll back the merge. Foreign-key pragma settings are restored. SQLite
+operations that explicitly roll back the enclosing transaction retain that effect.
+
+Concurrent destination writes and simultaneous snapshots across separate source
+files are outside this interface's contract. The CLI equivalent is
+:ref:`merge-preserving-keys <cli_relational_merge>`.
+
